//? Title: Undercomplete Autoencoders: Learning through a Bottleneck
//? Subtitle: A hand-worked nonlinear reconstruction, its parameter derivatives, and a visible Adam fit of the original 784–32–784 network on genuine MNIST
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Compressing an observation into fewer coordinates loses information unless the data have structure.
An undercomplete autoencoder learns an encoder and a decoder together, using the original observation
as its reconstruction target. This report derives the mean squared reconstruction objective, checks a
two-feature, one-coordinate example and every parameter derivative, then trains a 784–32–784 network
on genuine 28 by 28 MNIST images. Validation reconstruction error selects the checkpoint; a separate
held-out pool measures reconstruction against initial, black, fitting-mean, zero-code and shuffled-code
baselines. The selected network obtains held-out MSE 0.05033, compared with 0.06780 for the fitting
pixel mean. The example is deliberately small and does not establish a general-purpose image compressor.
)
\toc

> The method

An observation \(x\in[0,1]^d\) is mapped to a code \(h\in\mathbb R^k\), where \(0<k<d\), and back to a
reconstruction \(\hat x\). Here the encoder is ReLU and the decoder is sigmoid:

$$
a=xW_e+b_e,\qquad h=\max(a,0),\qquad z=hW_d+b_d,\qquad \hat x=\sigma(z).
$$
\alttext(The encoder applies an affine map and ReLU. The decoder applies an independent affine map and a sigmoid.)

The two weight matrices are independent: \(W_e\in\mathbb R^{d\times k}\) and
\(W_d\in\mathbb R^{k\times d}\). This gives \(2dk+k+d\) learned scalars. The code constrains the
forward information path, but the decoder still stores learned information in its parameters.
A real compression format would also specify quantization, bits, metadata and a decoder shared with
the receiver; a reduction from 784 real values to 32 real values alone does not specify a bit rate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/undercomplete_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(55, 1100);
  diagram.node("x", {0, 0}, "Observation x\nd features")
      .node("h", {5, 0}, "Code h\nk < d coordinates")
      .node("r", {10, 0}, "Reconstruction\nd sigmoid outputs")
      .node("loss", {5, -3}, "Mean squared error\nCompare reconstruction with x")
      .edge("x", "h", "Affine + ReLU")
      .edge("h", "r", "Affine + sigmoid")
      .edge("x", "loss", "Target")
      .edge("r", "loss", "Prediction")
      .text({5, 2}, "Adam updates both affine maps using reconstruction gradients")
      .save_svg("build/undercomplete_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/undercomplete_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The bottleneck belongs to the prediction path. The target is the input itself; digit labels play no role in fitting.)
\alttext(An observation passes through a smaller code into a reconstruction. Both observation and reconstruction feed the mean squared error.)

For a batch \(X\in\mathbb R^{N\times d}\), the objective averages over both observations and features:

$$
L=\frac{1}{Nd}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{d}(\hat X_{ij}-X_{ij})^2.
$$
\alttext(The objective is the sum of squared reconstruction residuals divided by N times d.)

Let \(D=2(\hat X-X)\odot\hat X\odot(1-\hat X)/(Nd)\), and
\(E=(DW_d^\top)\odot\mathbf1\{XW_e+b_e>0\}\). The chain rule gives

$$
\nabla_{W_d}L=H^\top D,\qquad \nabla_{b_d}L=\sum_iD_i,\qquad
\nabla_{W_e}L=X^\top E,\qquad \nabla_{b_e}L=\sum_iE_i.
$$
\alttext(Decoder weight gradients are H transpose D. Encoder weight gradients are X transpose E. Bias gradients sum the corresponding deltas over rows.)

At an encoder kink the ReLU derivative needs a convention; the numerical derivative check below uses
strictly positive activations. A forward and reverse pass each require \(O(Ndk)\) arithmetic, with
\(O(dk+N(d+k))\) parameter and activation storage. Training is nonconvex: neither Adam nor a low
validation loss guarantees a globally optimal representation. The fitted function need not be a
principal-component projection because both maps here are nonlinear.

The public `dm::nn::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder` now owns the reusable forward model. The caller
owns its `ParameterStore`, optimizer, batching, checkpoint selection and evaluation. Parameter names
remain `encoder/W`, `encoder/b`, `decoder/W` and `decoder/b`, preserving existing checkpoints.

> A two-feature example, checked by hand

Take \(X=((0.1,0.8),(0.7,0.2))\), \(W_e=(0.3,0.2)^\top\), \(b_e=0.1\),
\(W_d=(0.4,-0.5)\) and \(b_d=(-0.2,0.1)\). The two codes are 0.29 and 0.35.
The first decoder logits are \((-0.084,-0.045)\), and the second are \((-0.06,-0.075)\).
Applying the sigmoid gives all four predictions. The program compares their squared residuals
and all seven analytic parameter derivatives with autograd.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/undercomplete_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  n::ParameterStore p(17, type);
  const auto x = n::tensor({.1, .8, .7, .2}, {2, 2}, type);
  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, x, 2, 1);
    p.set("encoder/W", n::tensor({.3, .2}, {2, 1}, type));
    p.set("encoder/b", n::tensor({.1}, {1}, type));
    p.set("decoder/W", n::tensor({.4, -.5}, {1, 2}, type));
    p.set("decoder/b", n::tensor({-.2, .1}, {2}, type));
  }
  const auto result = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, x, 2, 1);
  const auto loss = n::mse(result.reconstruction, x);
  const auto gradients = n::grad(loss, p.parameters());
  const auto delta = (result.reconstruction - x) * result.reconstruction *
                     (1. - result.reconstruction) / 2.;
  const auto encoder_delta = n::matmul(delta, n::transpose(p.at("decoder/W")));
  const std::vector<n::Tensor> hand{
      n::matmul(n::transpose(x), encoder_delta), n::sum(encoder_delta, {0}),
      n::matmul(n::transpose(result.code), delta), n::sum(delta, {0})};
  std::print("| parameter | autograd | chain rule | maximum absolute difference |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < gradients.size(); ++i) {
    const double error = n::max(n::abs(gradients[i] - hand[i])).item();
    if (error > 1e-13) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.2e} |\n", p.names()[i], gradients[i].values_str(), hand[i].values_str(), error);
  }
  std::print("\nCodes: {}. Reconstructions: {}. MSE: {:.9f}.\n",
             result.code.values_str(), result.reconstruction.values_str(), loss.item());
  if (n::max(n::abs(result.code - n::tensor({.29, .35}, {2, 1}, type))).item() > 1e-13) return 1;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(60, 1050);
  d.node("x0", {0, 0}, "x = (0.1, 0.8)").node("h0", {5, 0}, "h = 0.29")
      .node("r0", {10, 0}, n::select(result.reconstruction, 0, 0).values_str())
      .node("x1", {0, -2}, "x = (0.7, 0.2)").node("h1", {5, -2}, "h = 0.35")
      .node("r1", {10, -2}, n::select(result.reconstruction, 0, 1).values_str())
      .edge("x0", "h0").edge("h0", "r0").edge("x1", "h1").edge("h1", "r1")
      .save_svg("build/undercomplete_toy.svg");
}
```
