> Checking the bottleneck restriction

Away from ReLU kinks, the input Jacobian factors through \(k\) coordinates. Its rank is at most
\(k\), so a strict bottleneck cannot have the identity derivative on an open subset of
\(\mathbb R^d\). This is a local smoothness statement, not a claim that it cannot memorize finitely
many training images. A highly flexible network can still assign distinct codes to a finite sample.
For the same two-feature model, this program checks the Jacobian determinant at three positive-activation inputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  n::ParameterStore p(17, type);
  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, n::zeros({1, 2}, type), 2, 1);
    p.set("encoder/W", n::tensor({.3, .2}, {2, 1}, type));
    p.set("encoder/b", n::tensor({.1}, {1}, type));
    p.set("decoder/W", n::tensor({.4, -.5}, {1, 2}, type));
    p.set("decoder/b", n::tensor({-.2, .1}, {2}, type));
  }
  std::print("| input | Jacobian rows | determinant |\n|---|---|---:|\n");
  for (const auto values : std::vector<std::vector<double>>{{.1, .8}, {.7, .2}, {.4, .4}}) {
    const auto x = n::from_vector(values, {2});
    const auto jacobian = n::jacobian([&](const n::Tensor& input) {
      return n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, input.reshape({1, 2}), 2, 1)
          .reconstruction.reshape({2});
    }, x);
    const auto j = jacobian.to_vector<double>();
    const double determinant = j[0] * j[3] - j[1] * j[2];
    if (std::abs(determinant) > 1e-14) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.2e} |\n", x.values_str(), jacobian.values_str(), determinant);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input | Jacobian rows | determinant |
|---|---|---:|
