| [0.7, 0.2] | [[0.029973, 0.019982], [-0.0374473, -0.0249649]] | 0.00e+00 |
| [0.4, 0.4] | [[0.0299521, 0.019968], [-0.0374766, -0.0249844]] | 1.08e-19 |
// result_end

> The original MNIST architecture

The numerical study keeps the source example's 784–32–784 geometry, ReLU, sigmoid, independent
weights and Adam learning rate 0.003. The executed architecture below uses a single-row shape probe,
Flow style and layer detail. It displays 50,992 learned scalars, with inference recording enabled
only for tracing. Tensor dimensions use the diagram defaults; this flow view does not display
one glyph per pixel or code coordinate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/undercomplete_architecture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  n::ParameterStore p(1471);
  n::Module model([&](const n::Tensor& input) {
    return n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, input).reconstruction;
  }, "Undercomplete autoencoder: 784 to 32 to 784");
  n::ModelPlotOptions options;
  options.style = n::ModelPlotStyle::Flow;
  options.detail = n::ModelPlotDetail::Layers;
  const auto diagram = model.plot(n::zeros({1, 784}), options);
  diagram.save_svg("build/undercomplete_architecture.svg");
  if (p.count() != 50992 || diagram.parameter_count() != p.count()) return 1;
  std::print("{} parameter tensors, {} learned scalars.\n", p.size(), p.count());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
4 parameter tensors, 50992 learned scalars.
\image(figures/undercomplete_architecture.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The network that is actually trained: 784 features, 32 ReLU coordinates and 784 sigmoid reconstructions.)
\alttext(A left to right tensor architecture showing the encoder dense layer, bottleneck activation and decoder dense layer.)

> Genuine MNIST: fit, select, then evaluate

`prepare_example_data("mnist-assets", data_home())` acquires verified original MNIST and prepares
balanced pools of 1,000 fitting, 200 validation and 200 official test images. `LoadSaliency` checks
byte ranges, original row IDs, balanced classes and distinct pixel records across the pools. Pixels
are Float32 values divided by 255. Labels are used only to arrange the final gallery. No downloaded
checkpoint or Python preparation is needed.

The explicit loop below makes three passes over the fitting pool, minibatches of 32, seed 1483.
The reported training loss averages each minibatch's pre-update MSE with its actual size, including
the partial last minibatch. Validation MSE after each epoch selects the saved weights. The held-out
pool is then scored using that selected checkpoint. These small pools and three epochs describe a
quick demonstration, not a full MNIST benchmark.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/undercomplete_learning.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::filesystem::path cache = dm::datasets::data_home();
  dm::datasets::prepare_example_data("mnist-assets", cache.string());
  const auto pools = n::models::LoadSaliency(cache / "saliency");
  const auto fit = pools[0].images.reshape({1000, 784});
  const auto validation = pools[1].images.reshape({200, 784});
  const auto test = pools[2].images.reshape({200, 784});
  n::ParameterStore p(1471);
  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, n::zeros({1, 784}));
  }
  const auto initial_test = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, test).reconstruction.detach();
  const auto pixel_mean = n::mean(fit, {0}, true);
  n::OptimizerConfig config;
  config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
  n::Optimizer optimizer(p, .003, config);
  n::BatchOrder batches(1000, 32, 1483);
  const auto probe = n::take_rows(fit, {0, 1});
  const auto first_gradients = n::grad(n::mse(n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, probe).reconstruction, probe), p.parameters());
  std::print("| parameter | initial gradient L2 norm |\n|---|---:|\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < first_gradients.size(); ++i) {
    const double norm = n::sqrt(n::sum(n::square(first_gradients[i]))).item();
    if (!std::isfinite(norm) || norm <= 0) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.7f} |\n", p.names()[i], norm);
  }
  double best = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  int selected = 0;
  std::vector<double> epochs, training, valid;
  std::print("\n| epoch | fitting pre-update MSE | validation MSE |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int epoch = 1; epoch <= 3; ++epoch) {
    batches.begin_epoch();
    double total = 0;
    std::size_t seen = 0;
    for (;;) {
      const auto rows = batches.next();
      if (rows.empty()) break;
      const auto input = n::take_rows(fit, rows);
      const auto output = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, input);
      const auto objective = n::mse(output.reconstruction, input);
      const double value = objective.item();
      if (!std::isfinite(value)) return 1;
      total += value * rows.size();
      seen += rows.size();
      optimizer.minimize(objective);
    }
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    const double score = n::mse(n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, validation).reconstruction, validation).item();
    if (seen != 1000 || !std::isfinite(score)) return 1;
    if (score < best) {
      best = score;
      selected = epoch;
      p.save("build/undercomplete_best.npz");
    }
    epochs.push_back(epoch);
    training.push_back(total / seen);
    valid.push_back(score);
    std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", epoch, total / seen, score);
  }
  p.load("build/undercomplete_best.npz");
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto output = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, test);
  const auto zero_code = n::broadcast_to(n::sigmoid(p.at("decoder/b")), test.shape());
  std::vector<std::int64_t> shifted;
  for (int i = 0; i < 200; ++i) shifted.push_back((i + 1) % 200);
  const auto shuffled = n::sigmoid(n::matmul(n::take_rows(output.code, shifted), p.at("decoder/W")) + p.at("decoder/b"));
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, n::Tensor>> predictions{
      {"initial network", initial_test}, {"black", n::zeros_like(test)},
      {"fitting pixel mean", n::broadcast_to(pixel_mean, test.shape())},
      {"selected autoencoder", output.reconstruction}, {"zero code", zero_code}, {"cyclically shuffled codes", shuffled}};
  std::print("\nSelected epoch: {}. Validation MSE: {:.9f}.\n\n| held-out predictor | MSE over 200 images |\n|---|---:|\n", selected, best);
  for (const auto& [name, prediction] : predictions)
    std::print("| {} | {:.9f} |\n", name, n::mse(prediction, test).item());
  n::save_npz("build/undercomplete_results.npz", {{"test_input", test}, {"test_reconstruction", output.reconstruction},
      {"test_code", output.code}, {"test_labels", pools[2].labels}, {"test_ids", pools[2].source},
      {"fit_ids", pools[0].source}, {"validation_ids", pools[1].source},
      {"pixel_mean", pixel_mean}, {"initial_test", initial_test}, {"shuffled_test", shuffled}});
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(epochs, training, "Fitting, pre-update", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines(epochs, valid, "Validation, post-epoch", {220, 38, 38}, 2.)
      .x_label("epoch").y_label("mean squared pixel error").title("Three epochs of Adam, learning rate 0.003")
      .save_svg("build/undercomplete_learning.svg");
}
```
