\alttext(A two-row contact sheet with original digits ordered zero through nine above their corresponding reconstructions.)

> Conclusion

A strict bottleneck constrains the rank of the smooth reconstruction map. The hand example checks
how the encoder and decoder share the reconstruction gradient. The MNIST program exposes every
optimization update and uses validation alone for checkpoint selection; the held-out baselines and
gallery qualify what three small-data epochs learn. Pixel MSE rewards average intensity agreement,
which can favour smooth or blurred reconstructions. No classification, latent disentanglement,
rate-distortion optimum or full-data benchmark follows from this experiment.

> References

- Goodfellow, I., Bengio, Y. and Courville, A. (2016). *Deep Learning*, section 14.1,
  [Undercomplete autoencoders](https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/autoencoders.html).
- LeCun, Y., Bottou, L., Bengio, Y. and Haffner, P. (1998). Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition.
  [Proceedings of the IEEE 86(11), 2278–2324](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791).
